  "output": "## Reality check first\n\nThis repo isn't a typical app codebase — it's the **JARVIS Obsidian vault** (personal/business OS in Markdown) plus one real piece of software: `index.html`, a ~5,300-line vanilla JS single-page app (\"TALES OF TALLES\" fitness tracker) that persists to `localStorage`. There's no database, no ORM, no React. So I mapped your five categories to their closest real analogues and only reported what I actually found in the code — no invented findings.\n\n### 1. \"N+1 query\" analogues — repeated full-scan reads\n\n**`index.html:6569-6571`** — `getNutC()` does `JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(...))` (deserializing the *entire* nutrition cache) on every single lookup, and `setNutC()` does a full read-modify-write of that same blob per key written:\n```js\nfunction getNutC(){try{return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(NK)||'{}')}catch(_){return {}}}\nfunction setNutC(k,d){try{const c=getNutC();c[k]={d,ts:Date.now()};localStorage.setItem(NK,JSON.stringify(c));}catch(_){}}\nfunction getNutE(k){const c=getNutC();const e=c[k];return(e&&Date.now()-e.ts<NUT_TTL)?e.d:null}\n```\nIf `getNutE`/`setNutC` are called per food item in a loop, this is the classic N+1 shape — N parses/serializes of the same object instead of 1. Fix: load once into a module-level object, mutate it in memory, and flush to `localStorage` on a debounce or on `pagehide`.\n\n**`00 JARVIS/🤖 JARVIS.md:128-130`** — the dashboard's `dataviewjs` block calls `dv.pages()` three separate times, each re-scanning the whole vault's metadata cache:\n```js\nconst tarefasAtrasadas = dv.pages().file.tasks.where(...).length;\nconst projetosAtivos   = dv.pages().where(p => p.tipo == \"projeto\" ...).length;\nconst lastCorporal     = dv.pages().where(p => p.tipo == \"corporal\").sort(...).first();\n```\nFix: `const all = dv.pages();` once, then derive all three from `all`. Same fix applies in `70 Sistema/Automacao/🔁 Automacoes.md:340-342`, which re-filters `dv.pages()` per financial metric.\n\n### 2. Re-render analogue (no React, but same disease)\n\n**`index.html:4406-4445`** — `renderAll(state)` unconditionally re-renders ~13 independent sections (header, camp, corner, arsenal, rotina, stats, tips, equipment, weight chart, measures, connections, meal diary, chapters) on *every* call, regardless of which slice of state actually changed. It's called ~10 times across the file after nearly any mutation (workout toggle, meal log, scan save, event dispatch, undo, etc.) — e.g. `index.html:3712,3728,4077,4692,4892,5861,5878,6714,7805`.\n\nThis is the vanilla-JS equivalent of a React component with no `memo`/`shouldComponentUpdate`: one field changing (e.g. toggling a workout override) repaints the weight chart, muscle heatmap, and meal diary too. Fix: split `renderAll` into targeted `render<Section>(state)` calls invoked only by the mutations that touch that section, or at minimum diff a cheap hash of the relevant sub-state before repainting expensive bits (canvas chart, heatmap).\n\n### 3. Redundant computation\n\n**`index.html:2885-2900` (`gruposEvolucao`) called from `rHeatmap` at `5697`, itself invoked from `renderAll:4443`** — every render (while the S&C page is open) does `Object.values(LIVE.exDone||{}).forEach(...)` over the *entire* exercise history to recompute muscle-group evolution percentages, then re-sorts and rebuilds the DOM rows from scratch:\n```js\nfunction gruposEvolucao(mus){\n  const workCount={};\n  Object.values(LIVE.exDone||{}).forEach(rec=>{ (rec.muscles||[]).forEach(m=>{workCount[m]=(workCount[m]||0)+1;}); });\n  ...\n}\n```\nAs `exDone` grows over months of training log, this becomes an O(n) recompute on every repaint tick instead of an O(1) incremental update. Fix: maintain `workCount` as a running tally updated incrementally when an exercise is logged, instead of rebuilding it from the full history each render.\n\n**`index.html:4412-4429`** — the \"TELEMETRIA\" block inside `renderAll` filters `Object.values(LIVE.exDone||{})` again (`exAll`, `exWeek`) — a second full-history scan on the same render pass that could share the single pass done in `gruposEvolucao`.\n\n### 4. Memory leak risk\n\n**`index.html:3908` / `4088`** — two `setInterval`s (`_cdInterval` for countdown, `_decayInterval` for the 5-minute decay tick) are started but I found no matching `clearInterval` paired with them outside the `pagehide` handler at `4092`. Worth confirming that handler actually clears both — if the app ever re-initializes without a full page reload (e.g., a future SPA-style route swap), these intervals would stack.\n\nNo other real leak patterns (dangling large closures, unbounded caches, detached DOM accumulation) turned up — event listeners are attached once during a single init pass, not inside re-render loops, so they don't multiply on each `renderAll`.\n\n### 5. Caching opportunities\n\n- **Nutrition cache** (`getNutC`/`setNutC`, `index.html:6569`): move from \"parse whole blob every call\" to an in-memory cache hydrated once at load, as above.\n- **Dashboard Dataview queries**: Obsidian's Dataview already caches file metadata, but the vault MOCs each independently call `dv.pages()` and re-filter — consolidating per-file into a single `dv.pages()` call per dataviewjs block avoids redundant iteration on every note re-render (Dataview reruns these blocks on any vault write).\n- **`gruposEvolucao`**: cache its result keyed on `LIVE.exDone` length/last-updated timestamp, invalidate only when a new exercise is logged.\n\n### What doesn't apply\nNo backend, no database ORM, no React component tree — so literal N+1 SQL queries, React `useMemo`/re-render profiling, and server-side caching layers (Redis, etc.) aren't relevant here. The findings above are the closest real equivalents that actually exist in this codebase.\n",
